      "headline": "BIOTECH: SANOFI'S INSMED DEAL, A CEILING ON RATES AND THE PATENT CLIFF POINT TO MORE MONEY IN THE SECTOR",
      "html": "<p>Sanofi has agreed to buy Insmed. A deal of this size puts a lot of money back into biotech: the cash goes to Insmed's shareholders, and much of it is likely to be reinvested in the sector.</p><p>Rates look to have hit a ceiling. Oil spreads are coming down, so inflation may start to ease, which could lead to rate cuts further into 2027.</p><p>Large pharma companies also face a patent cliff and need to replace the sales they will lose. Buying biotech companies is the quickest way to do that.</p><p>Together, I think these will lead to excess liquidity in biotech.</p><h2>The Insmed deal</h2><p>Sanofi is paying $175 a share in cash, $41.0 billion in all, which is 46% above Insmed's share price before the first reports of a bid. It is the first drug-company takeover above $30 billion since Pfizer bought Seagen in 2023, and more than three times the size of the next largest this year.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"0\"></div></figure><p>It lands in a year that was already busy. IQVIA counted $130 billion of drug-company takeovers in the first half of 2026, almost as much as the $133 billion in the whole of 2025.</p><p>Biotech shares were ahead of the market before the deal. The biotech index (XBI) is up 29% this year against 5% for the S&amp;P 500, and it rose 6.6% on the day the deal was announced.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"1\"></div></figure><p>The cash itself arrives when the deal closes, which Sanofi expects in the first half of 2027. Until then the effect is on prices and expectations.</p><h2>Rates, oil and inflation</h2><p>This is the part of my view that the market does not share. The Fed raised its target range to 3.75% to 4.00% in September. It held in October, but two of the twelve voters wanted another rise, and futures put the chance of a rise at the 9 December meeting at about 95%.</p><p>On my side, Treasury yields have come off their highs. The 10-year yield peaked at 5.31% on 5 October and was 4.92% on 23 November. The 2-year peaked at 4.92% in late September and was 4.63%.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"2\"></div></figure><p>Oil is the reason I expect that to continue. Brent crude averaged $124 a barrel in October and was $93 on 17 November. The spreads have fallen further than the price. Brent's premium over WTI, the US benchmark, averaged $22 in October and $3 in November so far. The refining margin, which is what lifts petrol and diesel prices above the price of crude, averaged $68 in September and was $33 on 17 November.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"3\"></div></figure><p>Inflation this year has followed energy. Consumer prices were rising 2.4% a year in January, more than 4% in May and 3.7% in September. Over the same months the measure that leaves out food and energy stayed between 2.5% and 2.9%. If fuel keeps getting cheaper, the headline rate should follow it down. The bond market leans the same way: the inflation rate priced into five-year Treasuries is 2.1%, its lowest in a year.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"4\"></div></figure><p>The turn is not in the published figures yet. October's inflation was estimated at about 4.1%, higher than September, and diesel has fallen much less than crude. EY's chief economist expects inflation to stay high into 2027. Rate cuts in 2027 are my forecast, not what the market prices today.</p><h2>The patent cliff</h2><p>Evaluate estimates that drugs with about $300 billion of yearly sales lose patent protection between 2025 and 2030. The largest wave, $104 billion, comes in 2028, the year Keytruda's main patent expires. The ten largest drugs account for $126 billion of the total.</p><figure><div class=\"pchart-slot\" data-k=\"5\"></div></figure><p>Sanofi is an example. Dupixent brings in about two-fifths of its revenue and loses patent protection in 2031, and the company has said openly that it needs acquisitions to replace it. Insmed is the largest one so far.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li>Sanofi, <a href=\"https://www.sanofi.com/en/media-room/press-releases/2026/2026-11-24-06-30-00-3409002\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">press release on the Insmed acquisition</a>, 24 Nov 2026.</li><li>Fierce Pharma, <a href=\"https://www.fiercepharma.com/pharma/sanofi-inks-41b-insmed-buyout-post-dupixent-growth-drivers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sanofi inks massive $41B Insmed buyout for post-Dupixent growth drivers</a>, 24 Nov 2026.</li><li>IQVIA, <a href=\"https://www.iqvia.com/locations/emea/blogs/2026/07/biopharma-ma-mid-year-2026-update\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Biopharma M&amp;A: mid-year 2026 update</a>, 8 Jul 2026. It counts takeovers of $250 million or more.</li><li>BioBucks, <a href=\"https://www.biobucks.co/biotech-ma-tracker-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Biotech M&amp;A tracker 2026</a>, updated 16 Nov 2026.</li><li>Federal Reserve, <a href=\"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FOMC statement</a>, 28 Oct 2026.</li><li>24/7 Wall St., <a href=\"https://247wallst.com/investing/2026/11/18/traders-now-see-a-95-chance-the-fed-hikes-rates-in-december/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Traders now see a 95% chance the Fed hikes rates in December</a>, 18 Nov 2026.</li><li>EY, <a href=\"https://www.ey.com/en_us/insights/strategy/macroeconomics/cpi-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consumer Price Index October 2026</a>, 10 Nov 2026.</li><li>Evaluate, <a href=\"https://content.evaluate.com/story/portfolio-tactics-to-scale-the-dollar300bn-patent-cliff/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Portfolio tactics to scale the $300bn patent cliff</a>.</li><li>Rates, oil prices, consumer prices and expected inflation: Federal Reserve, EIA and BLS figures from <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FRED</a>. Share prices: Yahoo Finance.</li></ul>",
