      "html": "<p>On 2 November I closed the first trade: I sold the shares at $51.95 and bought the $55 call back at $3.08. The shares had fallen 5% since May, but the call made back more than that, so the trade gained $2.80 a share, or 5.1%.</p><p>On 3 November Fiserv reported its third quarter. Sales stopped falling after a weak first half, the 2026 forecast was held, and share buybacks restart this quarter. The shares rose 12% on the day.</p><p>That is the first evidence the turnaround is working, so I reopened with a higher strike: shares at $56.00, and the $60 call expiring 19 March 2027 sold for $4.75. This leaves $4.00 of room for the shares to rise, against $0.31 before.</p><p>The premium still cushions a fall to $51.25, about where the shares were before the results, in case the improvement turns out to be a fluke. The most this trade can make is $8.75 a share, or 15.6%.</p>"
